all right let's talk a little bit about  um uh  personal finance i guess in a sense  let's talk about the national savings  rate uh as a result of the pandemic and  sort of some other  related topics and we'll sort of build  up to uh what some are calling kind of  like new asset allocations that make  sense for the average person  nothing in this video is is financial  advice just as a reminder we're just  talking about  some of these issues there's a very  interesting article in the atlantic by  robert shapiro called what the u.s  loses when americans save too much and  it points out  accurately that during the pandemic  americans sucked more away than at any  time  in recent memory and that that's a  problem  um what the uh article argues  is that quote as the pandemic recedes  the economy faces a serious new  challenge  the personal savings rate remains high  14.9  in april 12.4 percent in may with large  unemployment benefits and waves of free  cash now starting to recede  in the country's rearview mirror the  continuation of that high level of  personal savings threatened a strong  expansion into 2022 and 2023.  this is basically a demand side economic  perspective  the article goes on to say because a  dollar saved is a dollar  unspent any sudden enormous jump in  personal savings would normally  devastate consumer spending and economic  growth  fortunately both the trump and biden  administrations  managed to get the economics of the  pandemic right the united states averted  a sustained economic catastrophe  by pumping up people's incomes with  emergency payouts  while savings rates were soaring  the article goes on to say the final  result is that even with gdp and job  numbers cratering in the second quarter  of 2020  and the highest savings rate in 75 years  the growth in personal incomes from all  sources including checks from the  government  accelerated a lot during the pandemic  total personal income increased by 1.4  trillion that's 7.6 percent  those income gains outpaced the 4.4  average annual income growth from 2015  to 2019. so  the idea here is and this is a that you  know when we talk about  why is it that tax cuts for the really  rich aren't that good for the economy  it's for the same reason that robert  shapiro is arguing that people saving a  lot of money  isn't that great for the economy uh  which is that  if you have a low marginal propensity to  consume to spend  meaning you're really rich you don't  need any additional money to fund your  lifestyle and somebody gives you a  little more money  the odds that you spend that and insert  it into the economy so it will circulate  and multiply through an economic  multiplier is very low you'll sock it  away in a bank account more likely  that's the same demand side concern if  americans overall are  saving much more are participating  in an activity not spending that is not  particularly economically stimulative  and that is a that that is a really big  deal at a macroeconomic level the other  thing that's sort of going on  as all of this is happening and i don't  pretend to know what the impact of it is  going to be  is that more and more legacy financial  firms  and you can like them or not you can  trust them or not but a lot of people  pay attention to them  are starting to say that the average  person could or even  in some cases should make cryptocurrency  a part of their portfolio  jpmorgan for example says they they said  it months ago  investors could make bitcoin one percent  of portfolios  there are other firms that talk about  three to four percent  i've said before not financial advice  that  from my instinct my belief after owning  bitcoin for  i don't know uh five six years at this  point  i think it's clearly not going away and  so i'm like a  four to five percent of my total assets  type of guy  as that is happening you could see the  combination  of a high savings rate as well as saving  some of that money in cryptocurrency  which could lead to a dramatically  different  sort of economy three to five years from  now i don't pretend to know exactly the  way in which it will be different but  there's no question that that certainly  would  uh and that sort of relates actually to  a platform  that uh i've been trying out lately that  i'll take a second just to tell you  about uh this  the platform is called bit levex this  video was  sponsored by bit levex after we reached  out to them we partnered up on this  video  they have a really unique product it's  basically options with leverage  i've never really traded options before  i've been wanting to just  dip my toe in a little bit find out how  they're priced and so on  and i was surprised to find out that  there's a really fascinating model  behind it called the black and shoals  model which won a nobel prize  in economics so i tried the bit levex  platform i was impressed with how easy  it was  i did a little bit of trading you can  have a look at some of my results the  profit percentages that you see there  so this could be a useful tool if you're  involved in trading and you want to work  quickly  there's leverage available up to 100 the  options can be sold back after five  minutes  so i can control risk from several  angles including  leverage and time to maturity one thing  i really liked about  levex is the personal attention to every  user you have a personal manager you can  talk to on the phone  if you want they'll talk to you about  how to trade options how to manage risk  it's all free had a really interesting  and informative conversation  they have options on you know all the  popular assets tesla stock amazon stock  netflix bitcoin others so you can do  both traditional and crypto assets on  one platform  the withdrawals are fast you can do  paypal which makes it convenient  deposits are commission free which is  not that common  user-friendly platform lots of hands-on  support  and i put a link to bitlovex under the  video if you want to check it out and  again as always do your due diligence  never invest what you're unwilling to  lose i'm not giving financial advice  uh i am just telling you about my  experience with the bit levex  platform which it and platforms like it  are increasingly being adopted by people  who are saying  let me do what jpmorgan is saying or let  me do what whoever is saying  let me try putting one percent two  percent sometimes three percent whatever  makes sense for your sort of risk  profile and appetite  into alternative currencies digital  currencies cryptocurrencies  i don't know anything about anything my  my approach is basically time  in the market beats timing the market  and that's why  with my stocks and bonds i'm a buy and  hold guy  with my cryptocurrencies i've been  mostly a buy and hold guy just selling  individual tranches to lock in some  gains  and i think we're increasingly going to  see that particularly again because of  the combination of the high savings rate  as well as the accessibility of a lot of  these new platforms

